ca is a C++ library written without the standard library, without exceptions and without
RTTI, for programs that want every cost visible: no hidden allocation, no hidden failure, no libc
or std name reaching the user. Errors are values (`ca::result`), resources are released
explicitly (`cleanup()`), memory comes from an allocator each type names, and whatever cannot fail
in a correct program traps instead of throwing.

### Layers

The library is built in six ordered layers. A layer depends only on the layers below it, which
the include paths make visible (`#include "3-system/file/open.hpp"`), and each layer page of this
reference opens with what its layer is for.

| Layer | Contents |
|---|---|
| 1-primitives | the language and the compiler: traits, integers, bits, atomics, hashing, memory primitives, traps |
| 2-vocabulary | value types: `result`, `optional`, `error`, views (`span`, `borrowed_slice`, `sv`), unicode, I/O contracts |
| 3-system | the OS boundary: `ca::sys` wrappers, `ca::syscall`, `errc`, error traces |
| 4-lifecycle | creation and destruction: allocators, arena, heap, `make`, `unique`, contexts |
| 5-collections | containers, strings, maps, lists, paths, formatting, in-memory I/O, reports |
| 6-resources | descriptors, buffered I/O, stdout and stderr, `print` |

Programs include the facade instead of the layer paths: `#include "ca/print.hpp"`, or
`"ca/all.hpp"` for everything.

### A first program

```c++
#include "ca/dynamic_array.hpp"
#include "ca/error.hpp"
#include "ca/flush.hpp"
#include "ca/hashmap.hpp"
#include "ca/print.hpp"
#include "ca/str.hpp"
#include "ca/sv.hpp"

[[nodiscard]] static auto count_words(const ca::sv::str text, ca::hashmap<ca::str, int>& counts) noexcept
	-> ca::result<ca::usz> {
	ca::usz total = 0U;
	for (const ca::sv::str word : text.split()) {
		auto slot = counts.setdefault(word.strip(".,"), 0);
		if (!slot)
			return slot.propagate();
		++**slot;
		++total;
	}
	return total;
}

auto main(void) -> int {
	ca::hashmap<ca::str, int> counts{};
	auto total = count_words("the cat saw the dog. the dog ran.", counts);
	if (!total)
		return 1;
	if (!ca::print("{} words, {} distinct, 'the' x{}\n", *total, counts.size(), counts["the"]))
		return 2;
	return counts.cleanup() && ca::flush() ? 0 : 3;
}
```

The habits it shows run through the whole library: every allocation is a `result` to check
(`setdefault` may grow the map), views (`sv::str`) are passed by value and never allocate, map
lookups by text need no temporary string, formats are checked when the program compiles, and the
program releases what it owns and checks that its output was written before it exits.

### Building

`make` builds `build/<compiler>-<mode>/libca.a` (clang++ debug by default; `CXX=g++`,
`MODE=release`), `make test` runs the tests, `make matrix` checks both compilers in both modes,
and `make gen` regenerates the generated sources and this reference. A program compiles with
`-std=c++2c` (clang) or `-std=c++23` (gcc), `-nostdinc++ -fno-exceptions -fno-rtti`, `-I src/lib`,
and links `libca.a`.
